{"title":"1979 USAMO Problems/Problem 5","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#Problem\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#Solution\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#Solution_2\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 2</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-4\"><a href=\"#See_Also\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">4</span> <span class=\"toctext\">See Also</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1979_USAMO_Problems/Problem_5&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/e/5/2e551daed3a63b8e5cdb40ab6a6fc9cc0fb71b5a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A_1,A_2,...,A_{n+1}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"118\" height=\"17\" /> be distinct subsets of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/d/e/0deff9086fd7840ef23860f5bc924f1d4d2d2310.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$[n]$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"19\" height=\"18\" /> with <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/a/a/7aa851fbfa07ff125e01310437a2b6ff2f70a536.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$|A_1|=|A_2|=\\cdots =|A_{n+1}|=3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"238\" height=\"18\" />.  Prove that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/e/6/9e6869876278f38955b42fccb7de045782bf0b95.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$|A_i\\cap A_j|=1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"102\" height=\"18\" /> for some pair <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/6/e/b6e42edf269f5f93f439001f1fa019924bb5c1ff.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\{i,j\\}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"40\" height=\"18\" />.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution\">Solution</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1979_USAMO_Problems/Problem_5&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Suppose the problem statement does not hold. It is clear that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/1/b/d1b94ea0cc3ac2d353695f157196aed47896fefc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n \\ge 4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"44\" height=\"14\" />. Choose the smallest <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> such that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/b/3/2b37c4bd495c9ba42c62c843e26062c371315584.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$|A_i \\cap A_j| \\neq 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"102\" height=\"18\" /> for each <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/5/d/65dc619a741a6cf9eda2ddc0a628e2b72712042d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\{i, j\\}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"40\" height=\"18\" />.\n</p><p>First, the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/9/c/99cb3f3a8b3bbfc702c5863d16602a06cded535d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(n+1)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"54\" height=\"18\" /> subsets have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/0/8/508a827da036a8bfc486df697a64a48387a055ef.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3(n+1)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"64\" height=\"18\" /> elements (some repeated) in conjunction. Because there are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> elements of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/d/e/0deff9086fd7840ef23860f5bc924f1d4d2d2310.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$[n]$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"19\" height=\"18\" /> total, by the Pigeonhole Principle one element of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/d/e/0deff9086fd7840ef23860f5bc924f1d4d2d2310.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$[n]$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"19\" height=\"18\" />, say <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/3/8c325612684d41304b9751c175df7bcc0f61f64f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"14\" />, is in at least four subsets. Let these subsets be <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/4/c/04c42ee5b0fb229e8708441e6be436a8c0082752.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A_1, A_2, A_3, A_4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"107\" height=\"16\" />, without loss of generality, and let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/6/c/36c7f3f828cf12ab9cafeffad282468a95219600.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A_1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"23\" height=\"17\" /> have elements <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/a/e/9ae457ae89047895b4516b6052975e123b08457a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k, m, n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"52\" height=\"16\" />. Then without loss of generality let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/3/a/e3a8ae66ded7f33f9385a37c9cadfb82720013d8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A_2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"19\" height=\"15\" /> have elements <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/3/1/63131c31d57596573fbb8a89de75dbb1c4287ac5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k, m, p$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"51\" height=\"16\" />. If set <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/9/1/791fe65c9d1ed7d8ed33a5025f40c5c7f9e476af.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A_3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"19\" height=\"15\" /> has elements <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/e/e/ceec022fdd4641bc8665173a7f6713861530a465.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k, n, p$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"46\" height=\"16\" />, then simple casework shows that it is impossible to create <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/b/6/3b6f936abe426272c6ed7300a218f88be89175a4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A_4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"20\" height=\"15\" /> without having <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/b/6/3b6f936abe426272c6ed7300a218f88be89175a4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A_4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"20\" height=\"15\" /> intersect one of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/e/d/9ed2eabd48aeefe6f4846a274328691e09811212.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A_1, A_2, A_3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"78\" height=\"16\" /> at exactly one element. Thus assume <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/9/1/791fe65c9d1ed7d8ed33a5025f40c5c7f9e476af.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A_3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"19\" height=\"15\" /> has elements <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/2/7/627f78528a96f5e434ad353153614c35b89d718b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k, m, q$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"50\" height=\"16\" />. Then <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/b/6/3b6f936abe426272c6ed7300a218f88be89175a4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A_4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"20\" height=\"15\" /> has elements <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/1/3/513c6c3b0f78b930826014697347c76513733400.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k, m, r$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"50\" height=\"16\" />. Consider each remaining set <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/7/4/2744600189f7f1df31756f2022b2d5212ef1c767.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A_i$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"18\" height=\"15\" />. Then <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/7/4/2744600189f7f1df31756f2022b2d5212ef1c767.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A_i$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"18\" height=\"15\" /> either contains both <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/9/a/79a52121ce59dbbf7b474fea33c7299f7d699dff.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k, m$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"33\" height=\"16\" /> or none of them. Because there are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/6/6/566aa684bcb55b4c157e26cb0a7faa94d391c3d1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(n-2)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"54\" height=\"18\" /> distinct elements of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/d/e/0deff9086fd7840ef23860f5bc924f1d4d2d2310.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$[n]$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"19\" height=\"18\" /> apart from <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/9/a/79a52121ce59dbbf7b474fea33c7299f7d699dff.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k, m$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"33\" height=\"16\" />, at most <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/6/6/566aa684bcb55b4c157e26cb0a7faa94d391c3d1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(n-2)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"54\" height=\"18\" /> subsets can contain <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/9/a/79a52121ce59dbbf7b474fea33c7299f7d699dff.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k, m$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"33\" height=\"16\" />. Then at least 3 subsets do not contain <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/9/a/79a52121ce59dbbf7b474fea33c7299f7d699dff.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k, m$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"33\" height=\"16\" />, and it is easy to see that they are disjoint from those subsets that do contain <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/9/a/79a52121ce59dbbf7b474fea33c7299f7d699dff.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k, m$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"33\" height=\"16\" />. Thus, we can partition <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/d/e/0deff9086fd7840ef23860f5bc924f1d4d2d2310.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$[n]$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"19\" height=\"18\" /> into two subsets, one of which is the union of the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/0/d/e0d2bf360290fd61d1c1557e763f2622363b3d35.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$t$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"8\" height=\"13\" /> subsets that do contain <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/9/a/79a52121ce59dbbf7b474fea33c7299f7d699dff.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k, m$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"33\" height=\"16\" />, and the other is the union of the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/a/e/aaecf4952775023cccd893babccc495d73df30d1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(n+1)-t$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"87\" height=\"20\" /> subsets that do not contain <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/9/a/79a52121ce59dbbf7b474fea33c7299f7d699dff.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k, m$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"33\" height=\"16\" />. Because the latter subset has <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/5/3/0531d76b8d9a80994945acf3fccafb4030368ebe.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(n-t-2) &lt; (n+1)-t - 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"225\" height=\"18\" /> elements, we may use infinite descent to contradict the minimality of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" />. The proof is complete.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_2\">Solution 2</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1979_USAMO_Problems/Problem_5&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 2\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/d/2/ad28c83c99a8fd0dd2e2e594c9d02ee532765a0a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$S$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"14\" height=\"14\" /> be a collection of distinct subsets of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/d/e/0deff9086fd7840ef23860f5bc924f1d4d2d2310.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$[n]$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"19\" height=\"18\" /> such that for all <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/6/5/a651051db64d634b61290a7e08390e107686e7b4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A_i, A_j \\in S, i \\neq j$\" style=\"vertical-align: -6px\" width=\"130\" height=\"19\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/e/3/be3d5977579e61694680eee649836dfdfb61c923.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$|A_i| = 3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"64\" height=\"20\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/b/3/2b37c4bd495c9ba42c62c843e26062c371315584.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$|A_i \\cap A_j| \\neq 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"102\" height=\"18\" /> (in other words, either <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/7/4/f7491f91a0fdbf716c5ac937d443d8a1fb8f1b2e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$|A_i \\cap A_j| = 2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -6px\" width=\"105\" height=\"20\" /> or <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/a/3/6a3dc489c02b75af62a1ec21b7b2724e4765fd2f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$|A_i \\cap A_j| = 0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -6px\" width=\"105\" height=\"20\" />). We will find an explicit solution for the maximal size of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/d/2/ad28c83c99a8fd0dd2e2e594c9d02ee532765a0a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$S$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"14\" height=\"14\" />, which will be strictly less than <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/e/3/0e3efd9b14723a92c2ae891fe27780d5f8e2b215.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n+1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"44\" height=\"15\" />.\n</p><p>Let's define the relation <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/5/5/e55156a4008b0944ad00d5bc71bc5aa6315aabb7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\sim$\" style=\"vertical-align: 2px\" width=\"13\" height=\"5\" /> on <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/d/2/ad28c83c99a8fd0dd2e2e594c9d02ee532765a0a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$S$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"14\" height=\"14\" /> as <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/0/3/d036880eb418fea127bf9fc5d5500ca956040611.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A_i \\sim A_j \\iff |A_i \\cap A_j| \\ge 2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -6px\" width=\"223\" height=\"20\" />. Notice that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/0/5/105399570d71f06839a9e96320840e181381180a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A_i \\not\\sim A_j \\iff |A_i \\cap A_j| = 0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -6px\" width=\"223\" height=\"20\" />. Suppose <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/5/6/956e9ae70428e85fc349ae6120c9539ab7d30581.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A_i \\sim A_j$\" style=\"vertical-align: -6px\" width=\"66\" height=\"19\" />. Notice that if <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/5/5/7553256f94a2ce1069471dfe7c43bf8b7215ab90.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A_k$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"24\" height=\"17\" /> shares 2 elements with <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/7/4/2744600189f7f1df31756f2022b2d5212ef1c767.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A_i$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"18\" height=\"15\" />, at least one of those elements must also be in <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/6/e/b6edb286660f22d694ff4152ede3c948864cbb99.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A_j$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"19\" height=\"17\" />, and thus <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/5/5/7553256f94a2ce1069471dfe7c43bf8b7215ab90.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A_k$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"24\" height=\"17\" /> must share 2 elements with <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/6/e/b6edb286660f22d694ff4152ede3c948864cbb99.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A_j$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"19\" height=\"17\" /> as well. so either <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/6/e/56e56721b969f83c00613a4efc53f28f43ff83f2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$|A_i \\cap A_k| = |A_j \\cap A_k| = 2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -6px\" width=\"203\" height=\"20\" /> or <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/a/1/ba1f193d7b3e4ce734a309414be6faa07a54c0e4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$|A_i \\cap A_k| = |A_j \\cap A_k| = 0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -6px\" width=\"203\" height=\"20\" />. Thus <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/9/d/e9d7761161d845b6ff0c038c4e8f5ff63fb94b63.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A_k \\sim A_i \\sim A_j \\implies A_k \\sim A_j$\" style=\"vertical-align: -6px\" width=\"228\" height=\"19\" />, so <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/5/5/e55156a4008b0944ad00d5bc71bc5aa6315aabb7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\sim$\" style=\"vertical-align: 2px\" width=\"13\" height=\"5\" /> is transitive. Observe that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/5/5/e55156a4008b0944ad00d5bc71bc5aa6315aabb7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\sim$\" style=\"vertical-align: 2px\" width=\"13\" height=\"5\" /> also satisfies reflexivity and symmetry. We can think of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/5/5/e55156a4008b0944ad00d5bc71bc5aa6315aabb7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\sim$\" style=\"vertical-align: 2px\" width=\"13\" height=\"5\" /> as an equivalence relation on <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/d/2/ad28c83c99a8fd0dd2e2e594c9d02ee532765a0a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$S$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"14\" height=\"14\" />, and thus its equivalence classes partition <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/d/2/ad28c83c99a8fd0dd2e2e594c9d02ee532765a0a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$S$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"14\" height=\"14\" />. Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/4/e/54e758dc62b189ef6d8fab9ea2ed576393dc5318.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$C_1,...,C_p$\" style=\"vertical-align: -6px\" width=\"74\" height=\"19\" /> be the equivalence classes of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/d/2/ad28c83c99a8fd0dd2e2e594c9d02ee532765a0a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$S$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"14\" height=\"14\" /> under <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/5/5/e55156a4008b0944ad00d5bc71bc5aa6315aabb7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\sim$\" style=\"vertical-align: 2px\" width=\"13\" height=\"5\" />. Since no elements are shared between subsets in different equivalence classes, the unions <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/2/7/2271c8025e0b94bfff249f9b95f3d12a3104793b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\bigcup C_1,..., \\bigcup C_p$\" style=\"vertical-align: -11px\" width=\"120\" height=\"31\" /> are disjoint. Suppose for some <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/4/8/34857b3ba74ce5cd8607f3ebd23e9015908ada71.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$i$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"8\" height=\"14\" /> that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/1/4/6148f44cbd408997385083ddf2dcd6576102adae.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$|\\bigcup C_i| = m$\" style=\"vertical-align: -11px\" width=\"96\" height=\"31\" />. For <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/8/c/08ccf69785a6dfe808272cf75362a876c43247ed.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m\\neq 4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"51\" height=\"18\" />, the maximal number of distinct subsets in <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/1/1/c117e8403b3b9cf74d2f63f6627aa17c1995e3bc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$C_i$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"17\" height=\"15\" /> is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/0/f/a0f555deaf69c02efa1021189cc6d4c05db35886.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m-2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"49\" height=\"15\" />, as you first choose two elements which will be shared across all the subsets in <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/1/1/c117e8403b3b9cf74d2f63f6627aa17c1995e3bc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$C_i$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"17\" height=\"15\" /> and then one distinct element for each of those subsets. In the special case of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/0/0/500fb9788ed8553a319dd49cb6262faebcacc88b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m=4$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"49\" height=\"12\" />, the maximal number of subsets in <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/1/1/c117e8403b3b9cf74d2f63f6627aa17c1995e3bc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$C_i$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"17\" height=\"15\" /> is 3 because you can use a triangular structure like <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/9/4/a94a6186776d1ebe07729697e80ba640b8e84fa3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\{1,2,3\\}, \\{1,2,4\\}, \\{2,3,4\\}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"203\" height=\"20\" />, but it is fairly obvious that you cannot use a similar strategy to get more than <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/0/f/a0f555deaf69c02efa1021189cc6d4c05db35886.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m-2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"49\" height=\"15\" /> for other values of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/5/0/f5047d1e0cbb50ec208923a22cd517c55100fa7b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"10\" />. In any case, considering that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/9/8/d98ad6337d99a8ca4f2ce82923238cce73b7112c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$|\\bigcup C_1| + ... + |\\bigcup C_p| \\leq n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -11px\" width=\"209\" height=\"31\" />, the best strategy to maximize the overall number of subsets is to have all the subsets in a single equivalence class. Thus, the maximal size of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/d/2/ad28c83c99a8fd0dd2e2e594c9d02ee532765a0a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$S$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"14\" height=\"14\" /> is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/f/9/ef9b02e32cfba0db0f9038a8d077596720f62e91.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n-2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"44\" height=\"15\" />, except for when <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/9/c/39cf3e35a4981583288d2a7c4b34989916fb7360.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n=4$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"44\" height=\"12\" /> in which case it is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/b/d/4bdca93962a2beaffc172ca394e04fe05bd74ffd.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n-1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"44\" height=\"15\" />.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_Also\">See Also</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1979_USAMO_Problems/Problem_5&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4\" title=\"Edit section: See Also\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<table class=\"wikitable\" style=\"margin:0.5em auto; font-size:95%; border:1px solid black; width:40%;\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td style=\"background:#ccf;text-align:center;\" colspan=\"3\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1979_USAMO\" title=\"1979 USAMO\">1979 USAMO</a></b> (<b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1979_USAMO_Problems\" title=\"1979 USAMO Problems\">Problems</a></b> • <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"http://www.artofproblemsolving.com/Forum/resources.php?c=182&amp;cid=27&amp;year=1979\">Resources</a>)\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Preceded&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1979_USAMO_Problems/Problem_4\" title=\"1979 USAMO Problems/Problem 4\">Problem 4</a></b>\n</td>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Followed&#160;by<br /><b>Last Question</b>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"3\" style=\"text-align:center;\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1979_USAMO_Problems/Problem_1\" title=\"1979 USAMO Problems/Problem 1\">1</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1979_USAMO_Problems/Problem_2\" title=\"1979 USAMO Problems/Problem 2\">2</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1979_USAMO_Problems/Problem_3\" title=\"1979 USAMO Problems/Problem 3\">3</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1979_USAMO_Problems/Problem_4\" title=\"1979 USAMO Problems/Problem 4\">4</a> <b>•</b> <a class=\"mw-selflink selflink\">5</a>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"3\" style=\"text-align:center;\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=USAMO_Problems_and_Solutions\" title=\"USAMO Problems and Solutions\"> All USAMO Problems and Solutions</a></b>\n</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<div class=\"print\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;width:100%;justify-content:space-between;align-items:center\">\n<div>\n<p><span class=\"mw-default-size\" typeof=\"mw:File\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=File:AMC_Logo.png\" class=\"mw-file-description\"><img src=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/d/d1/AMC_Logo.png\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"97\" height=\"84\" class=\"mw-file-element\" /></a></span>\n</p>\n</div>\n<div style=\"flex-grow:1;text-align:right\">\n<p>These problems are copyrighted &#169; by the <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://www.maa.org\">Mathematical Association of America</a>. \n</p>\n</div>\n<div style=\"clear:both;\"></div>\n</div></div>\n<!-- \nNewPP limit report\nCached time: 20261017173145\nCache expiry: 86400\nReduced expiry: false\nComplications: [show‐toc]\nCPU time usage: 0.042 seconds\nReal time usage: 0.064 seconds\nPreprocessor visited node count: 507/1000000\nPost‐expand include size: 1237/2097152 bytes\nTemplate argument size: 51/2097152 bytes\nHighest expansion depth: 3/100\nExpensive parser function count: 0/100\nUnstrip recursion depth: 0/20\nUnstrip post‐expand size: 16624/5000000 bytes\n-->\n<!--\nTransclusion expansion time report (%,ms,calls,template)\n100.00%   11.831      1 -total